<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ariobarzanes I Philoromaios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariobarzanes I Philoromaios was king of Cappadocia from about 95 to 63 BCE, chosen with Roman approval after the Ariarathid line ended. He was repeatedly driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> and his ally Tigranes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and restored by Roman commanders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. He abdicated in favour of his son Ariobarzanes II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariobarzanes-i-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ariobarzanes,_minted_at_Mazaca_in_83_or_82_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
